              {
                "text": "I'm examining the 7x7 grid structure with border markers, then mapping the inner 6x6 grid's values to figure out how the output transformation rearranges the rows into a new pattern.\n\nI'm noticing the marker positions (2 at col4 in row0, 2 at row3 in col0) might indicate split points for swapping quadrants, and I'm testing whether the output rows/columns are formed by exchanging blocks from the original grid's halves.\n\nI'm comparing the left three columns of the output against the left three columns of the input to see if there's a consistent row reordering rule I can pin down.\n\nContinuing the pattern, output row0 is input row4 with its two halves swapped, row1 is row5 swapped, and row5 is row3 swapped. Now checking row2: it looks like row2 stays the same, equal to input row2 itself with left and right halves matching (4 5 6 | 7 8 9), so no swap needed there.\n\nTrying a general cyclic offset formula instead, I test output[i][j] = input[(i+4)%6][(j+3)%6], and this checks out cell by cell against row0's values, matching 5,6,6,4,4,5 exactly to input row4 shifted by 3 columns.\n\nVerifying the remaining rows confirms the same shift of (4,3) works throughout - row1 matches input row5 shifted by 3, row2 matches input row0 shifted by 3, row3 matches input row1 shifted by 3, row4 matches input row2 shifted by 3, and row5 matches input row3 shifted by 3. So the whole grid is a cyclic shift by (4,3), and I'm now wondering if these shift amounts correspond to marker positions in the input - like a marker at column 4 in one row and a marker at row 3 in one column.\n\nTesting this idea against the first training pair, I look for the marker positions there too, getting a=3, b=1 candidates, but when I try applying that shift to the inner grid it doesn't match the actual output row0. So the marker-position theory doesn't hold cleanly, and I need to reconsider what's actually determining the shift amount.\n\nActually, maybe I'm mismatching which marker maps to which shift value\u2014reconsidering whether row marker determines b instead of a, and vice versa for column marker, since my current assumption isn't holding up across both examples.\n\nTesting this against the full grid coordinates: the marker's inner row index gives the down-shift amount, and the inner column index gives the right-shift amount, since shifting right by that amount is equivalent to shifting left by the complementary amount. Checking example 2 confirms this pattern matches both the row and column shifts I found earlier.",
